[2026-09-29 10:05] Sniff hint computed AFTER the vault write, never before.
why: It is a heuristic run on attacker-controlled input. Computing it before storage would mean a crafted log line could influence how that line was kept, which is the one thing the byte-exactness claim cannot allow. It is attached to the RawEvent on the way out. TestHintsReachDownstream asserts both halves: every record of a vendor's file sniffs the same way, and the stored bytes equal the source bytes regardless of what the sniffer decided.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/ingest.go, pkg/dataplane/ingest/sniff/

[2026-09-29 10:05] Sniffer is hand-rolled byte scanning, not regexp.
why: PRD 5.3's rules are expressible as regexes, and Go's RE2 is linear-time so it would be safe. But this runs on every record on the hot path, and the rules reduce to one pass over at most 256 bytes with no allocation. Measured: 132 ns/op, 0 allocs, ~1 GB/s on this machine. A regex-based version would be paid for by every event whether or not anything used the answer.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/sniff/sniff.go

[2026-09-29 10:05] A syslog-framed CEF or LEEF message is classified CEF or LEEF, not syslog.
why: PRD 5.3 rule 1 says so, and the reason is that the transport is incidental - the payload is the part a parser has to understand. Checked within 200 bytes of the PRI header.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/sniff/sniff.go

[2026-09-29 10:05] Expected hint per fixture is asserted, and three fixtures are deliberately "unknown".
why: multiline.log (ISO-dated stack traces), dhcp.log (ISC dhcpd prose) and openvpn.log have no machine-readable shape at a glance. Returning unknown is the honest answer; inventing a classification for them would mislead the parser registry into trying an extractor that cannot work. All 7810 fixture lines were also checked to return a valid hint and never panic.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/sniff/sniff_test.go

[2026-09-29 10:10] client_ca means RequireAndVerifyClientCert, never VerifyClientCertIfGiven.
why: Optional client authentication is the worst of both worlds - it reads as mutual TLS in the configuration and authenticates nobody. If an operator sets client_ca they mean it.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tls.go

[2026-09-29 10:10] A client_ca file containing no parseable PEM is a startup error.
why: Go's CertPool silently accepts an empty pool, and the listener would then reject every client. That surfaces as a network problem being chased for an afternoon rather than as the configuration error it is.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tls.go

[2026-09-29 10:10] FINDING: mutual TLS cannot be tested by checking whether the connection opened.
why: Under TLS 1.3 the client completes its half of the handshake before the server has validated the client certificate, so a client with NO certificate sees Dial, Handshake and its first Write all succeed; the rejection arrives as an alert on a later read. Verified against a live daemon: a Go client with no certificate reported "ACCEPTED (TLS 1.3)" and its record never reached the vault, while the authenticated client's did. The security property holds - but a naive test would conclude mutual TLS is broken when it works, or worse that it works when it does not. The unit tests write AND read; documented at the top of TLSConfig so the next person does not have to rediscover it.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tls.go, tls_test.go

[2026-09-29 10:10] `ingestd certs` generates ECDSA P-256, not RSA.
why: Far faster handshakes, universally supported by anything speaking TLS 1.2+, and small enough that the PEM files are readable. Serials are random 128-bit rather than sequential: sequential serials leak how many certificates have been issued and collide across independent runs. Private keys are written 0600 and the directory 0700. A generated README sits next to the files saying they are development-only, rather than that warning living somewhere an operator has to go looking.
affects: cmd/ingestd/certs.go

[2026-09-29 10:12] M4.1 and M4.2 verified: make check ALL GREEN.
why: Chunk rule. Sniff: 30 rule cases, every fixture's expected hint, all 7810 fixture lines valid and panic-free, input-not-modified, window bound, 132 ns/op. TLS: records carried with OriginTLS, untrusted server refused, TLS 1.1 refused, mutual TLS accepting a valid client and refusing both no-certificate and wrong-CA clients, config validation. Verified end to end against a live daemon with certificates from `ingestd certs`.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/sniff/, pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tls.go, cmd/ingestd/certs.go

[2026-09-29 10:30] Peer map prefixes are masked at load; a peer matching nothing falls back rather than being dropped.
why: Two things that both look like "the feature silently does not work". netip.Prefix.Contains returns false for an unmasked prefix like 10.1.4.7/24, so without Masked() a perfectly reasonable config line matches nothing at all. And a device that starts sending before anyone adds its CIDR keeps the listener's own source_id: refusing it would drop real data over a bookkeeping omission. IPv4-in-IPv6 peers are unmapped, or a dual-stack listener would miss every IPv4 rule.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/peermap.go

[2026-09-29 10:30] Peer map is resolved once per TCP connection and per UDP datagram.
why: A connection has one peer for its lifetime, so resolving per record would be wasted work. A UDP socket hears from every device, so there is nothing to resolve once. Different costs, same answer.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/{tcp.go,udp.go}

[2026-09-29 10:35] BUG: Multiline.Next held the mutex across its blocking inner read, so the timeout could never fire.
why: Making Multiline safe for the source's timer goroutine, the obvious `m.mu.Lock(); defer m.mu.Unlock()` on Next was wrong in the worst possible way: Next blocks until a line arrives, so the lock was held for exactly as long as the source stayed quiet - and a quiet source is the ONLY situation the timeout exists for. The feature would have looked implemented, passed a busy-source test, and done nothing in the case it was written for.
Fixed by restructuring so the inner read happens outside the lock and the lock covers only the state mutation. Caught by TestMultilineTimeoutReleasesAHeldRecord, which writes an event and then nothing.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/multiline.go

[2026-09-29 10:35] The multiline timeout is checked inside FlushIfOlderThan, not by the source.
why: The source could read an age and then decide to flush, but the record's age and the decision to flush have to be read under the same lock or a line arriving in between produces a record flushed mid-append. Passing the duration in and letting the decoder decide keeps both under one lock.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/multiline.go, pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/file.go

[2026-09-29 10:35] The tailer's position fields are mutex-guarded now that two goroutines advance them.
why: The read loop and the multiline timeout goroutine both submit records, and both advance the offset the next checkpoint will record. Unguarded, a checkpoint could be written from a half-updated position - skipping or repeating a record on the next restart. The offset also only ever moves forward, so an out-of-order submit cannot rewind it.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/tail.go

[2026-09-29 10:40] M4.3 and M4.4 verified: make check ALL GREEN, race clean at -count=2.
why: Chunk rule. Peer map: longest prefix wins with deliberately unsorted config, unmasked prefixes, IPv4-in-IPv6, bare addresses, nonsense addresses, nil and empty maps, validation, and live TCP and UDP. Multiline timeout: a held record released when the source goes quiet, and an active record NOT cut while its continuations keep arriving.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/peermap.go, pkg/dataplane/ingest/frame/multiline.go

[2026-09-29 11:10] Metric source labels are capped at 50, folding into "other".
why: `source` is a metric label, and with dynamic_sources: true an HTTP client CHOOSES it. An unbounded label is an unbounded number of time series, which is how a Prometheus server is taken down by the thing it is monitoring. Past the cap everything folds into "other"; the records still carry their real source_id, so only the label collapses and nothing that matters is lost. Frame-error reasons come from a fixed set for the same reason - never from error text, which is network-influenced.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/metrics.go

[2026-09-29 11:10] A nil Registerer gives a working Metrics rather than nil.
why: Otherwise every recording site needs a nil check, and the one that gets forgotten is a nil dereference on the hot path. NewMetrics registers against a throwaway registry instead, so the write path is unconditional.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/metrics.go, pkg/dataplane/vault/metrics.go

[2026-09-29 11:10] Latency histogram buckets span 50us to 5s, not the Prometheus defaults.
why: The three regimes this system actually sees are sync=none writes in the low microseconds, an fsync to an SSD in the low milliseconds, and a struggling disk in the hundreds. The default buckets put all three in one bucket and say nothing about any of them.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/metrics.go

[2026-09-29 11:10] vault_failed is a gauge, not a counter.
why: A counter makes "it failed once an hour ago" and "it is failing right now" the same number. vault_failed is the only signal that the vault has stopped accepting writes entirely, and it is what an alert fires on.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/metrics.go

[2026-09-29 11:10] vault_put_latency_seconds is observed once per batch, not per record.
why: The latency a caller actually experiences is the batch's. Observing per record would make the histogram claim a thousand fast writes where there was one, which is the opposite of what a p99 is for.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/vault.go

[2026-09-29 11:12] ingest_udp_kernel_drops reports NaN where the platform cannot read it, not 0.
why: Caught by running the daemon on macOS: the gauge read a reassuring flat 0, which means "no datagrams were dropped" - on a platform that has no way to tell. That is exactly the quiet false assurance the UDP documentation is at pains to avoid. Prometheus renders NaN as absent, so a dashboard shows "no data" instead. Linux reads Udp: RcvbufErrors from /proc/net/snmp.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/source/{udp.go,drops_linux.go,drops_other.go}

[2026-09-29 11:12] The metric type label is derived from the record's Origin, not from configuration.
why: A configured label can disagree with reality - a source declared `tcp` that is actually receiving over TLS would be mislabelled forever. Deriving it from Origin.Kind means the label cannot be wrong about where the bytes came from.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/ingest.go

[2026-09-29 11:12] BUG: the new `kind` field raced between Submit and write.
why: Submit set stream.kind under s.mu, but write() reads it outside the lock, which is where the batch is actually sent. Caught immediately by -race. The kind now travels with the batch out of detach(), under the same lock that takes the records - the same shape as the earlier ordering fix.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/ingest.go

[2026-09-29 11:15] /metrics is served on the loopback metrics_addr, never on an ingest port.
why: Scraping and ingesting have different exposure. Devices reach the syslog and HTTP ports; nothing that reaches those should be able to read internal counters and queue depths.
affects: cmd/ingestd/main.go

[2026-09-29 11:15] scripts/send_samples.sh is POSIX sh using only nc, curl and jq.
why: It has to run inside the container, where there is no bash and no coreutils beyond the basics, and PRD 9.1 already assumes nc, curl and jq. sha256sum and shasum are both handled because GNU and BSD userlands differ. It exits non-zero on any failed check, so CI can run it as a gate rather than a human reading output.
affects: scripts/send_samples.sh

[2026-09-29 11:20] M4 COMPLETE. make check ALL GREEN, 2890 passing subtests, three direct dependencies.
why: Chunk rule. Sniff hint (advisory, computed after storage, 132 ns/op), TLS listeners with mutual TLS and `ingestd certs`, peer map, the multiline timeout, the full Prometheus metric set from PRD section 13 on a loopback /metrics, and scripts/send_samples.sh. The script was run against a live daemon and passed every check: all five ingest paths, verify --deep exit 0, three independent SHA-256s agreeing, a proof verifying with no vault present, and vault_failed at 0.
Remaining: M5 - compaction (.zst/.meta/.hix), the 200-cycle kill -9 suite, benchmarks, docs/vault-format.md. The kill -9 suite is what "0 acknowledged records lost" is measured on and still does not exist.
affects: pkg/dataplane/ingest/, pkg/dataplane/vault/, cmd/ingestd/, scripts/

[2026-09-29 11:45] 200-cycle kill -9 crash suite implemented. RESULT: 4417 acknowledged records, 0 lost.
why: This is the test behind the project's central claim, and it did not exist until now. A child process writes with sync=always and appends each acknowledged record to a log; the parent SIGKILLs it at a random moment between 15 and 75 ms, reopens the vault, and requires every logged record to be present byte-exact with a verifying chain. The vault directory is reused across all 200 cycles, so recovery runs against a real accumulated history - 4417 records, roughly 110 seals - rather than a fresh directory each time. It also asserts the vault grows every cycle, or a suite where recovery quietly discarded everything would pass vacuously. Runs in 18 seconds.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/crash_test.go

[2026-09-29 11:50] FINDING: the crash suite does NOT prove fsync works. Established by mutation, not assumed.
why: Two deliberate breakages were run against it. Reversing the seal order - appending the ledger line before fsyncing the footer - fails within 60 cycles with "segment 37 footer disagrees with its chain.log line", so the suite genuinely exercises the seal windows. But switching the child to sync=none, where an acknowledgement no longer means the record is on disk at all, PASSES.
That is not a gap in the test; it is the boundary of what killing a process can show. SIGKILL does not discard the page cache, so a record written with no fsync at all is still readable by the next process to open the file. The suite proves the recovery logic is correct - torn tails, interrupted seals, missing ledger lines, sequence contiguity - and it does not prove fsync put anything on a platter. Proving that requires cutting power, which no test can do.
This is recorded prominently because it is exactly the kind of thing a submission overclaims. "0 acknowledged records lost across 200 kill -9 cycles" is true and measured. "Durable" without qualification is not what it shows.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/crash_test.go, README.md, docs/vault-format.md (M5), the submission's claims section

[2026-09-29 11:50] The crash child does not fsync its receipt log.
why: SIGKILL does not discard the page cache, so the parent sees every line the child wrote without it. Fsyncing would double the I/O of the whole suite and prove nothing extra - and the one failure mode it would guard against, power loss, is explicitly not claimed anyway.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/crash_test.go

[2026-09-29 12:05] Every benchmark reports durable_ack as a metric alongside its number.
why: The three sync modes differ by orders of magnitude and only one means the acknowledgement was on disk. A records/s figure copied out of a terminal without its mode is worse than no figure, because it looks authoritative. durable_ack rides along with every throughput and latency result so the two cannot be separated in a spreadsheet.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/bench_test.go, Makefile

[2026-09-29 12:05] A TEST fails on non-Linux explaining that benchmark numbers are not reportable.
why: A comment does not stop anyone. Go's File.Sync issues F_FULLFSYNC on Darwin, which flushes the drive's own write cache, while Linux fsync usually returns once the data reaches the drive - the two differ by more than an order of magnitude and the PRD's targets are Linux targets. Measured here: 79k records/s at 64 producers in sync=always, p50 6ms. Those numbers are real and must not appear in the submission.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/bench_test.go

[2026-09-29 12:05] BenchmarkPutBatch varies producers (1, 16, 64), not just sync mode.
why: With one producer there is nothing for group commit to coalesce, so every batch pays its own fsync and the result is a latency measurement wearing a throughput costume. The 16- and 64-producer cases are the realistic ones and the reason group commit exists at all. On this machine the spread is 2.4k/s to 79k/s in sync=always - the same code, a 33x difference, purely from concurrency.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/bench_test.go

[2026-09-29 12:10] docs/vault-format.md written; section 10 is the one that matters.
why: It is the source for slide 3 and the two-page architecture document, and the reference for anyone writing a verifier or a tamper test. Section 10 ("What is NOT claimed") is deliberately the most detailed: power-loss durability is not proven and the reason is stated with the mutation result that established it; tamper-evident is not tamper-proof and the coherent-rewrite case is spelled out; UDP, at-least-once tail delivery and single-node are all named. A format document that only lists guarantees is how a submission ends up overclaiming.
affects: docs/vault-format.md

[2026-09-29 12:25] BUG FOUND BY THE CRASH SUITE UNDER -race: a vault could become unopenable.
why: openSegment creates the segment file, writes the 72-byte header and fsyncs. A crash inside those few microseconds leaves a file shorter than a header - a segment no record was ever acknowledged into. Recovery called that fatal corruption and refused to open the vault at all, so a kill landing in a microsecond-wide window of segment rollover produced a vault that could never be started again. That is strictly worse than losing the unacknowledged records: it loses everything.
It is now treated as never having existed - the file is removed and the id is free for the next write. Only the LAST segment qualifies; an empty segment in the middle still means a segment was removed, which no crash does.
Found at cycle 123, and only under -race, because the race detector slows the child enough to move where the kill lands. A 200-cycle suite that runs only without -race would not have found it. TestUninitialisedSegmentIsDiscarded now covers 0, 1 and 71 bytes directly rather than waiting for luck.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/recover.go, recover_test.go

[2026-09-29 12:30] The crash suite waits for the child to acknowledge something before killing it.
why: A fixed pre-kill sleep is simpler and wrong. `make check` runs every package in parallel, and under that disk contention - with macOS issuing a full drive flush per fsync - the child can fail to complete a single batch inside a fixed window, and the suite fails with "killed before it acknowledged anything". That is a flake about how busy the machine is, not a finding about the vault, and an intermittently failing crash suite is worse than none because it trains people to re-run it. It now polls for the first receipt and then kills after a random extra delay, so the kill point stays random where it matters - inside the write path - without the test measuring disk load.
affects: pkg/dataplane/vault/crash_test.go

[2026-09-29 12:30] `make check` runs a 40-cycle crash pass; `make crash` runs the full 200.
why: The crash suite found a real bug during an ordinary `make check`, so it earns its place in the main gate. 40 cycles adds about 25 seconds, which is acceptable for the gate that runs constantly; 200 stays behind `make crash` for a full pass.
affects: Makefile

