[2026-09-29 15:05] "Runs with no network" proven with the operating system, plus a permanent code-level guard.
why: A claim about the air gap should not rest on having read the code. ingestd and vaultctl were built from a fresh `git clone` with CGO_ENABLED=0, then run under sandbox-exec with `(deny network*)`. A control run first confirmed the sandbox really blocks (curl: "Could not resolve host"). With the network denied ingestd ingested 4000 records, compacted both segments (6.9x and 7.2x) and stopped cleanly, and vaultctl verified deep and returned bytes whose SHA-256 matched the source.
That is a point-in-time run. TestNothingDialsOut is the durable half: it parses every shipped non-test source file and rejects any call to net.Dial / tls.Dial / http.Get / http.Post and any import of net/smtp or net/rpc, allowing only the loopback --healthcheck probe. Proven able to fail by planting an http.Get in a scratch file, which it caught with file:line, then removing it.
Note this is macOS sandbox-exec; the Packaging workstream owns the container-level proof (`docker run --network none`, verify_airgap.sh).
affects: cmd/ingestd/egress_test.go

[2026-09-29 15:10] TWO GATE 1 ITEMS CANNOT BE MET FROM THIS MACHINE, and are reported as unmet rather than papered over.
why: (1) "Benchmarks recorded with hardware and sync mode": on macOS Go's File.Sync is F_FULLFSYNC, so every fsync-bound number is an order of magnitude off the Linux target; a test fails on non-Linux saying so. The benchmarks exist and run; the recorded Linux results do not. (2) "RSS at 100k records/s under 300 MiB, excluding hash index": needs Linux and a sustained load generator, and separately the in-memory per-record index is O(records), which is a documented limit rather than something this run can dismiss. Both are handed off, not claimed.
The compression RATIOS are the exception: they do not depend on the machine, so they ARE measured and reported (docs/vault-format.md 9.2).
affects: the submission's measured-numbers section, benchmarks/results/ (Packaging)

