[2026-09-29 21:40] B5 semantic typer built: intel/ulpf_intel/typer.py (file is typer.py, not the PRD's typing.py, which would shadow a stdlib module name). 58 pytest pass in total.
why: On palo_alto_unknown.log (500 headerless rows, 47 columns) all 7 manifest fields (src_ip, dst_ip, src_port, dst_port, proto, action, time) are typed to the right column and every value matches the manifest (7/7; bar 90%), and only columns the manifest confirms have confidence > 0.8. The drifted Fortinet keys (src, dst, sport, dport, proto, eventtime, action) also type correctly from values plus name hints. Ambiguity is shown, not hidden: NAT address col_9 and NAT-copy port col_27 are named in the evidence of the columns they could be confused with and lower their confidence.
Bugs found by looking at the output, not by the spec: (1) serial number 001801010001 typed as an epoch timestamp (leading-zero digit strings are ids); (2) Python's ipaddress.is_private includes the RFC 5737 documentation ranges the fixtures use for external hosts, so every address looked internal; now explicit RFC 1918/CGNAT/link-local like the identity package; (3) a constant "1" column typed as protocol ICMP; (4) a Python 3.13 DeprecationWarning for year-less strptime; tests now run with -W error.
Deviation: a headerless column's name term is neutral (0.5), not 0, so headerless columns are not capped at 0.75 by a term that says nothing about the values. Mutation-checked: is_private, leading-zero epoch, unflagged twin port and first-action-column tie-break each fail a test (the last only after I added a test whose data actually has two competing action columns; the manifest data does not).
affects: intel/
